Results (light and dark the same):
1. F3, a page that location.replace()s its clean address back to
   ?utm_source=loop: the server saw /replace, /replace (the rewrite),
   /replace?utm_source=loop (let through the second time), then nothing for
   3 s; the page settled, titled, painted. Before the fix the same page
   reloaded without end (582 requests in 10 s in the unit test).
1b. F6, a click on an anchor (#section) of that page: the URL gained
   #section, window.marker survived, no request reached the server (no
   reload without the parameters).
2a. F2, the user's Cmd-click (posted, .command) on a link: a second
   quarantine with its own store; the page stayed.
2b. The page's timer dispatching a Cmd-click (MouseEvent metaKey, no user
   gesture): no window. WebKit hands the policy no modifiers for a synthetic
   click, so the page followed it in place (/probe?n=timer).
2c. One real Cmd-click on a link whose click handler preventDefault()s and
   clicks another link five times: no window at all; the page navigated once
   to the other link in place. Scripted clicks inherit the gesture but not
   the modifiers, so a page cannot multiply the user's Cmd-click into windows.
3. F6, <a download href="/report.txt?utm_source=x">: the download question
   ("Download “report.txt”?" / "From 127.0.0.1, …", Cancel / Download); the
   request kept its parameters and its Referer (the download is not
   rewritten); Escape: no file, nothing listed.
4. Settings > Links: the Default caption with "except in Private windows",
   wrapping in four lines, light and dark.
Not verified at runtime: F5 (Open in when nothing takes the page: no way to
make the placement fail in the running app; unit test with a seam), F7/F8/F9
(pure rule and a one-line filter; unit tests), the loop without WebKit's
_isRedirect SPI (pure rule tests).

